{
  "comment": "The household. Ten people who have nothing to do with the mason's death and everything to do with whether the castle reads as lived in (BACKLOG.md rank 6, WISHLIST.md theme 1). None of them holds a clue, tells a lie or has a line: data/npcs.json's twelve are the mystery's, and these ten are the castle's. src/populace.js validates every field below against the plan's own walk grid before the page spawns any of them, and test/mystery.mjs runs the same validator in Node.",
  "routineComment": "A routine is a LOOP INSIDE A WATCH, not a timetable (#547, answer 3). The clock is the four bells the player rings and it does not move between them, so `prime` is a LIST of stops walked round and round until Terce. A list of one is somebody who stands still for the whole watch. A watch left out entirely is somebody who is not in the castle at that bell, and src/main.js hides the body, the same answer the merchant gets before Terce. A watch id is a bell of EITHER day: the four the mystery names and the walking day's own four, `prime-eve` to `vespers-eve` (see day0Comment).",
  "tileComment": "Every tile below is the centre of a real cell in the 0.5 m walk grid, which is why they read as multiples of 0.125 offset by 0.0625 rather than as round numbers. They were picked off the grid rather than guessed at, and the validator holds them there: floor under the stop, inside the room it names, 1.5 m clear of every other body at that bell, and a walk to the next stop.",
  "bodyComment": "Six bodies and a prop. Four of the bodies already worn by the twelve: `Woman.glb` for the five women (#603 put it in the cast the day this was written, and a populace woman in Farmer.glb would have been the thing that row exists to stop), `King.glb` with its crown hidden for the serjeant, and Farmer and Adventurer for the rest. `modelHeight` 1.65 goes with the women's pack, which is authored shorter than the men's. The fifth is `Hound.glb` (#644), Quaternius's Husky re-exported with its five materials named and its coat lifted so the tint is the colour, and nobody in the cast wears it. The sixth is `Hen.glb` (#684) and the prop is `Spear.glb` (#685), both Quaternius's off poly.pizza; see rank10bComment.",
  "rank10Comment": "The child and the hound are BACKLOG.md rank 10's first increment (#643 to #645). The child is not a child rig: she is Woman.glb at 1.15 m with `boneScale` putting her head a third larger, `clips.walk` naming Run and `speed` 2.2 so she runs between her stops, which is what a scaled-down adult looked like it was missing when the two were put side by side. The hound has `follow`: within 6 m of the player on a floor the grid can route to, it leaves its ring, trots to 1.8 m short of them and looks at them, and goes back to its stop when they are gone.",
  "rank10bComment": "Two hens and a spear are rank 10's second increment (#684 to #686). `Hen.glb` is Quaternius's Farm Animals chicken, fetched as glTF from poly.pizza because the pack itself ships none, re-exported with its five clips named and its one material `Feathers`; the atlas is the colour and the tint is a multiply over it, so the white hen's tint is near-white and the brown hen's is tawny. `peck` is its clip and exists in its body only. `Spear.glb` is Quaternius's too, a held prop under assets/NPCs rather than under polyhavenBase, which is what `heldPropPath` in src/populace.js decides; `heldPropFit` says it is 2.1 m long, gripped 40 % up from the butt, tip up, because npc.js's defaults are a mace's. The serjeant and the man-at-arms carry it and the archer does not.",
  "activityComment": "`activity` is the job, and src/populace.js's ACTIVITY_CLIPS maps it to a clip the body doing it ships. The nine standing jobs resolve to one of three idle variants, because the kit has no sweep, stir, hammer or spar in it. `sweep`, `stir`, `hammer`, `spar` and `drill` resolve to clips tools/bodies/ generates into the four human bodies (#788), and no routine below uses them yet. `sniff` and `eat` are the hound's (#643) and exist in its body only, and test/mystery.mjs checks each person's jobs against the clips in that person's own file.",
  "day0Comment": "THE WALKING DAY'S RINGS ARE THE FOUR WATCHES' RINGS, COPIED, AND THAT IS THE FLOOR AND NOT THE CEILING (SPECS.md's increment 4, #750 to #756). Every person below has `prime-eve` to `vespers-eve` under the same stops they walk at `prime` to `vespers`, because a walking day with no household in it is the emptiest this castle has ever been and because the cast's own day-0 schedule in mystery.json is written around these stops: src/populace.js validates every one of them against the grid and against the fourteen at the same bell, so a copy is a checked copy. What a copy does NOT say is that the household's evening is the same as its morning. The `talk` pairs below are still day-one only, so a `gossip` stop at a day-0 bell is two bodies standing still and saying nothing; a row that wants the household's own evening rewrites a ring here, widens the one `watches` test in src/populace.js's pair check, and costs nothing else.",
  "day2Comment": "Nobody here has a Lauds stop, so the morning after has the cast and nothing else. That is the increment's boundary and not a claim about the fiction; a later one gives the household a second day.",
  "gardenComment": "There is no gardener, and there was one until the grid was asked. `garden` is a room id in mystery.json that no point in this castle resolves to: there are 93 walkable cells east of the east gate and every one of them is inside the Chapel Tower's or the King's Tower's own disc, so `roomAt` names a tower and never the garden. A stop written there would be a stop in a room that does not exist on the ground. The hen-wife keeps the outer ward instead.",
  "rank6bComment": "The last five (#729): a page, the sacristan, the Lady's tiring-woman, a watchman and a writer, every stop of every watch in an inner-ward room, which takes what src/main.js builds to 32 bodies, exactly test/budget.mjs's MAX_SKINNED_TOTAL, and the inner ward's peak to 15 of 20 while the outer stays at 18. A sixth body is a ceiling argument in HISTORY.md, not a row here. The writer was to keep the muniment room and nothing in it is floor the validator accepts: the room is behind the riddle's lock, and the eight grid cells inside its disc all resolve to the King's Hall, so his three muniment stops stand in the inner ward outside the King's Tower instead, doing the same job.",
  "talkComment": "Two of the household talking where the player can overhear (#731, #732). The twelve's `chatter` pool in data/npcs.json is theirs and not this file's: every line in it starts with one of their names. A pair here is two ids from `people` in speaking order, one watch, and two to four lines alternating between them with no name in front, because the caption band carries the name. Each speaker has a ring of exactly ONE `gossip` stop at that watch, the two stops in one room from 1.5 to 3 m apart (src/populace.js's TALK_RADIUS), which is what keeps a pair standing still for the whole watch. The player hears it once per page, within 6 m (EARSHOT) and in the same room, on the band a sermon uses; a sermon out-ranks it. The lines are household talk, nothing about the mason, the death or the twelve's movements, because nothing reads a populace line against the timeline.",
